    "lawCode": "Amd §§6431 & 6432, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S2296\n\n TITLE OF BILL :\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to requiring campuses\nand colleges to provide incoming students with information about\ndomestic violence and stalking prevention\n\n\n PURPOSE OR GENERAL IDEA OF BILL :\nRequires college campuses to provide incoming students with\ninformation about domestic violence and stalking prevention.\n\n SUMMARY OF SPECIFIC PROVISIONS :\nSection 1.  Subdivision 3 of section 6431 of the educational law is\namended to include educating the campus community about domestic\nviolence and stalking offenses in addition to sexual assault.\n\nSection 6432 of the educational law is amended so that each college\nshall inform incoming students about domestic violence and staking\nmeasures in addition to sexual assault through programs which may\ninclude workshops, seminars, discussion groups and film presentations.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\nStudies indicate that at least one in five college students has\nexperienced domestic violence by a current or former dating partner.\nExperts also estimate that more than one in ten college women have\nbeen stalked by a boyfriend or ex-boyfriend. These statistics show\nthat domestic violence and stalking are significant risks to the\npersonal safety of college students. But in addition to threatening\nstudents' physical safety, these issues have significant negative\nimpacts on individual student victims' emotional well-being and\nacademic performance. Further, reports have identified domestic\nviolence or stalking as the precipitating factor in many recent\nincidents of campus violence, highlighting the significant public\nsafety risks these issues pose to campus communities.\n\nSections 6431 and 6432 of New York Educational Law Article 129-A\nrequire New York State institutions of higher education to address\nsexual assault through the provision of prevention information and\nthrough the work of advisory committees on campus security. This\nlegislation would put in place a similar requirement for addressing\ndomestic violence and stalking. The actions required by this\nlegislation will help prevent cases of domestic violence and stalking\nin the student population, thereby reducing the overall incidence of\ncampus violence and improving the health, well-being, and academic\ncapacity of New York's college students.\n\n PRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n2008:  A.10795 Passed Assembly\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\nNone to the state.\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nThis act shall take effect immediately\n",
